# Newline policy: none. Every file in this repository checks out byte for byte as it was
# committed, on every platform.
#
# This is not a style preference, it is a correctness requirement. `GliderPRO/` holds 1994
# Macintosh files that git's heuristics get wrong in both directions:
#
#   * `Glider PRO.r` is 15 MB of derez'ed resource fork with LF endings (199,843 of them) and
#     8-bit bytes throughout. With `text=auto` a Windows checkout would rewrite every LF to
#     CRLF, which changes the offsets `tools/extract_all.py` reads, changes the sha256s recorded
#     in `assets/extracted/art/manifest.json`, and so silently breaks `make assets-check` and
#     the fidelity corpus on exactly one platform.
#   * `Sources/*.c` and `Headers/*.h` are CR-only classic Mac text (see docs/DEV_ENVIRONMENT.md
#     §7). Nothing should "fix" them: they are the reference the port is transcribed from, and
#     they are vendored read-only.
#   * The 22 `.binhex` houses and 15 `.mov` movies are encoded data that happens to contain
#     newline bytes.
#
# The port's own files are covered by the same rule for the plain reason that gliderGo is
# developed and tested with LF and the fidelity tests hash bytes.
* -text

# Never diff, never merge, never guess at these. `binary` is `-text -diff`, restated per class
# so the intent survives someone relaxing the blanket rule above.
GliderPRO/Glider?PRO.r        binary
GliderPRO/Houses/*.binhex     binary
GliderPRO/Houses/*.mov        binary
*.png                         binary
*.wav                         binary
*.house                       binary

# GliderPRO/ is another author's 1994 material, vendored as reference and never compiled here.
# Only the data remains -- the C source is not redistributed, see README.md -- but the rule
# still earns its place: without it a forge's language statistics describe `Glider PRO.r`
# rather than the port, and its diffs are dominated by files no change to gliderGo will touch.
# It also covers Sources/ and Headers/ if someone drops upstream 94fed96's copies back in.
GliderPRO/** linguist-vendored

# The fidelity corpus is generated: reference PNGs and hash tables written by
# `go test ./internal/fidelity -update`. Reviewing them line by line is not useful; a moved
# hash is the signal, and the image is looked at with an image viewer.
internal/fidelity/testdata/** linguist-generated

# assets/extracted/ is committed so the game plays from a clone, but it is output:
# tools/extract_all.py produces every byte of it from GliderPRO/, and `make assets-check`
# proves it. Marked generated so it stays out of language statistics and out of diffs.
assets/extracted/**   linguist-generated
assets/extracted/**   binary
# The manifests are the exception worth reading in a diff: sha256 per extracted file, and the
# sound table. `diff` re-enables the textual diff that `binary` above turns off.
assets/extracted/manifest.json         diff
assets/extracted/art/manifest.json     diff
assets/extracted/sound/manifest.tsv    diff

# assets/extracted.zip is that same tree packed for //go:embed -- the copy every executable
# carries, so that a downloaded binary needs no files beside it. Generated, binary, and never
# merged: two branches that both ran `make assets-zip` have two archives of the same bytes and
# there is nothing to reconcile between them. `make assets-zip` is how it is regenerated and
# `go test ./assets` is what proves it matches the tree.
assets/extracted.zip  linguist-generated
assets/extracted.zip  binary
